hevc_rpivid: FFmpeg's HEVC decoder with the Raspberry Pi 4's rpivid block

A second FFCodec, "hevc_rpivid", is FFmpeg's HEVC decoder with the new "rpivid"
option on (0 off, 1 on, 2 also for coding tools not yet verified on the block;
-1, the default, is 1 for hevc_rpivid and 0 for hevc; FFMPEG_RPIVID=0|1|2 sets
it for hevc_rpivid). It is listed before "hevc" in allcodecs.c, so
avcodec_find_decoder(AV_CODEC_ID_HEVC) - what Haiku's media kit plugin calls -
returns it. It has no frame threads (slice threads only).

get_format(), once the software format is chosen, attaches the rpivid hwaccel
(libavcodec/rpivid_hevc.c, from the haiku-rpi repository's
patches/ffmpeg6/files): it decodes into the system-memory frames the decoder
allocates, so callers see a software decoder. A stream outside the block's
limits stays on the CPU. A picture the block cannot take, or one it fails,
moves the stream to the CPU decoder: the block's pictures lack the motion fields
the CPU decoder keeps for temporal MV prediction, so (with TMVP) pictures are
dropped up to the next IRAP; the block is taken again at an IRAP, three times
per stream at most. The hwaccel converts a picture while the block decodes the
next one: before the last pictures are output (an empty packet) it is asked to
convert the one it holds, and frames it marks AV_FRAME_FLAG_CORRUPT (the block
failed them after their submit) are dropped.

  configure      hevc_rpivid_decoder selects hevc_decoder
  Makefile       the rpivid objects
  allcodecs.c    ff_hevc_rpivid_decoder before ff_hevc_decoder
  decode.c       per-frame hwaccel data without a frames context
  hevcdec.[ch]   the option, the attach and fallback points, SAO buffers kept
                 for a fallback, the hevc_rpivid FFCodec

LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the files it changes.

